# Draco reliability audit — 11 October 2026

The review found and repaired failures in download integrity, media finalization, saved-state recovery, torrent deletion, command ordering, browser handoff, and renderer state. The final application passes **577 tests in 91 suites**, both TypeScript projects, production builds, Go bridge tests, packaged media-tool verification, packaged profile restoration, and packaged native handoff/replay.

This establishes tested behavior for the cases below. It does **not** establish that all possible bugs are gone. The remaining dependency advisories, engine limitations, and untested real-world conditions are listed explicitly.

## Scope and method

The review followed the authored main process, download engines, provider resolvers, preload/IPC contract, React renderer, Chromium extension, Firefox generation, Go native host, and packaging tools. It checked input admission through persistence, transfer, finalization, cancellation, deletion, restoration, and browser acknowledgements. Separate reviews covered desktop integration, media, and renderer behavior; the integration pass reviewed the changes and reproduced additional failures.

The [source manifest](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/docs/audit/2026-10-11/source-manifest.json>) records 192 source/configuration files, their hashes, and approximately 42,243 lines of authored code/markup/styles. It is an inventory of the review scope, not execution or branch coverage. Generated Firefox copies were checked against their generator; third-party source, binaries, image/font assets, and generated bundles were not reviewed line by line.

Tests were run before changes: **509 passed**. Reproductions were added for observed failures, and relevant existing tests were retained. The final total is **577 passed, zero failed, zero skipped**. Several fixtures execute bundled production modules with only Electron/provisioning dependencies replaced; they do not replace the code being tested. Media fixtures invoke the actual bundled FFmpeg and FFprobe. HTTP fixtures use real local servers. Renderer callback fixtures exercise component hooks and IPC actions without mounting a graphical Electron window.

The workspace has no Git metadata. Changes are local; no commit, pull request, installation, or publication was performed. A pre-edit source snapshot is available at [the audit backup](<C:/Users/Nihil/AppData/Local/Temp/draco-audit-bf21b9492d104c1a8fca9254be8c68b5.zip>). Audit evidence is retained in [the evidence directory](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/docs/audit/2026-10-11>).

## Download integrity and lifecycle findings

P1 indicates possible lost/corrupted data, unrelated-file deletion, or a process failure. P2 indicates a reproducible failure of a download or user command. P3 indicates misleading UI or another limited correctness issue. These are audit priorities, not a count of independent security vulnerabilities.

| Priority | Trigger and previous behavior | Corrected behavior and evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| P1 | HEAD supplied an obsolete length/validator, then GET returned a different chunked object. The runner could stop at the HEAD length and publish truncated output. | GET metadata controls the actual transfer; HEAD cannot supply a stale size or validator. Two real-server cases cover direct GET and ranged rejection followed by plain GET. [probe.ts:99](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/probe.ts:99>), [tests/probe-authority.test.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/tests/probe-authority.test.ts>). |
| P1 | Parseable journals could contain null segments, invalid boundaries/positions, or wrong metadata types and crash recovery or corrupt bookkeeping. | Journal loading validates the complete segment map and metadata before adoption. [journal.ts:51](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/journal.ts:51>), [tests/journal.test.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/tests/journal.test.ts>). |
| P1 | Conversion renamed the completed raw download into the destination before FFmpeg succeeded. A failed conversion lost the resumable source and left an unintended raw file. | Conversion takes place in the task workspace; the completed source survives failure/abort. Only a successful result is reserved and published. Real FFmpeg tests cover failure, playable MP3 output, collision preservation, and cancellation. [task.ts:834](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/task.ts:834>), [tests/task-postprocess.test.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/tests/task-postprocess.test.ts>). |
| P1 | Media cancellation/error promises could settle before the process had closed its output; cleanup could race a Windows file handle. | Conversion, mux, and MPD process paths wait for `close` and complete failure cleanup before reporting. [postprocess.ts:5](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/postprocess.ts:5>), [src/main/hls/mux.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/hls/mux.ts>), [src/main/dash/runner.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/dash/runner.ts>); conversion and media-reliability fixtures. |
| P1 | Torrent deletion recursively removed its root folder, including a user's unrelated files. | Deletion targets metadata-listed selected payloads beneath the download root; parent directories are removed only when empty. A bystander `notes.txt` survives the regression. [manager.ts:543](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/manager.ts:543>), [tests/torrent-output.test.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/tests/torrent-output.test.ts>). |
| P1 | A completed or seeding torrent could release its manager slot before WebTorrent closed its piece store. Removal then raced remaining handles/writes. | Completion and pause wait for teardown. Seed-stop promises are tracked and awaited by removal/shutdown; failed teardown cannot silently authorize deletion. [torrent.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/torrent.ts>), [manager.ts:842](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/manager.ts:842>); torrent-output and pause-failure regressions. |
| P2 | A custom torrent display filename disagreed with the actual metadata root; completion time could remain absent. | Metadata establishes the real root and locks the filename; completion records its timestamp. Filename edits are also rejected in main and disabled in dialogs. Torrent-output, IPC, and renderer regressions. |
| P2 | Resume during Pause's asynchronous flush could be overwritten by the settling old runner. Start during Remove could also restart data being deleted. | Pending Resume is applied after pause settles. Removal owns the task through teardown/deletion; scheduling excludes these tasks. [manager.ts:454](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/manager.ts:454>), [manager.ts:525](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/manager.ts:525>), [tests/manager-pause-failure.test.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/tests/manager-pause-failure.test.ts>). |
| P2 | A streaming redirect or rejected HTTP response could keep `body.dump()` waiting forever while bytes continued arriving. The idle timeout would not expire. | Unread bodies are destroyed immediately with their abort event handled. An endless redirect fixture reaches the destination within its deadline. [fetch-guard.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/fetch-guard.ts>), [src/main/engine/worker.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/worker.ts>), [tests/fetch-guard.test.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/tests/fetch-guard.test.ts>). |
| P2 | An unsupported Digest algorithm/qop could be chosen and answered incorrectly; changed realms could reuse another realm's credentials. Legacy `-sess` omitted its required cnonce. | Only supported challenge combinations are chosen; Basic can be used when offered, changed realms resolve their own credentials, and `-sess` sends cnonce. Auth, server, and complete-download tests pass. [auth.ts:96](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/auth.ts:96>), [tests/auth.test.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/tests/auth.test.ts>). |
| P2 | Reserved Windows names remained invalid with multiple extensions or superscript COM/LPT digits. Folder sanitizers appended an underscore after the extension, leaving the reserved prefix intact. | Files, category/group folders, and site paths escape the device prefix before the first dot. Naming, path, and site tests cover `CON.tar.gz`, `COM¹`, and `LPT²/³`. [naming.ts:14](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/engine/naming.ts:14>). |

## Media findings

| Priority | Trigger and previous behavior | Corrected behavior and evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| P1 | Fragmented MP4 HLS used the concat demuxer, which opened initialization data and fragments independently. Output could fail or omit frames. | Initialization data and all fragments are joined as one bounded-memory, cancellable input per track. Actual FFmpeg/FFprobe fixtures verify all 30 expected frames, including AES-128 encrypted initialization data. [runner.ts:184](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/src/main/hls/runner.ts:184>), [tests/media-reliability.test.ts](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/tests/media-reliability.test.ts>). |
