## Dependency findings

Compatible updates raised Electron from 43.4.1 to **43.7.9**, electron-builder to **26.17.0**, and the direct Undici dependency to **7.30.0**, with their applicable transitive fixes. Minimum package constraints and the lockfile were updated together. The downloaded Electron runtime was checked as well as package metadata.

`npm audit` decreased from **19 package findings (10 high, 9 moderate)** to **12 (4 high, 8 moderate)**. Production-only audit reports **4 high**. These counts include dependency ancestors; they correspond to **two remaining root advisories**, not twelve distinct vulnerabilities:

| Dependency | Path and remaining issue | Assessment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ip@2.0.1` | WebTorrent → torrent-discovery → bittorrent-tracker. [GHSA-2p57-rm9w-gvfp](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2p57-rm9w-gvfp) concerns incorrect public/private address classification. | The package remains in production dependencies. No call to the affected `ip.isPublic` was found in Draco or bittorrent-tracker; tracker server parsing uses `ip.toString`, and Draco creates clients. This is a reachability observation, not proof of absence of exposure. |
| `sprintf-js@1.1.3` | electron-builder → app-builder-lib → @electron/get → global-agent → roarr. [GHSA-hp3w-g68c-fv3c](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-hp3w-g68c-fv3c) concerns unbounded precision format strings. | Development/build dependency; absent from production-only audit. The advisory lists no patched release. |

The audit tool's forced alternatives downgrade WebTorrent to 0.7.3 or electron-builder to 26.5.0 and are marked as breaking changes. They were not applied. A dependency replacement or reviewed upstream fix remains required for a clean dependency audit. Exact registry responses are saved alongside the report.

## Remaining limits and release confidence

The [engine support matrix](<C:/Users/Nihil/Desktop/Github Repos/Draco/docs/ENGINE_SUPPORT.md>) remains the capability contract:

- MPD delegates remote media fetching to FFmpeg. It restarts from zero and refuses active speed caps/quotas. FFmpeg's redirect/subresource credential behavior is not protected by Draco's per-hop HTTP guard. Task-owned MPD fetching remains an architectural gap.
- Torrent caps apply per client; simultaneous clients do not share a true aggregate app/queue throttle. Torrent quotas are deliberately refused. Live swarm/private-tracker/passkey behavior was not exercised here.
- Reused HLS pieces and completed separate video/audio halves are identified by playlist/format identity and existence/size, without per-piece persisted digests. Same-size local corruption may escape reuse checks unless an expected final checksum is supplied. The fragmented-MP4 repair does not solve that integrity limitation.
- HTTP journal resume requires the existing strong-validator policy. A fixed signed Google media object has a separate bounded sequential-range path; changing URLs cannot safely reuse unverified bytes. HLS live/DRM and changing initialization maps remain unsupported and explicitly fail.
- Private-host checks classify URL hostnames; DNS rebinding and FFmpeg-owned requests were not penetration-tested. The origin-scoping regressions establish the tested header boundaries, not a comprehensive SSRF guarantee.
- The packaged bridge smoke exercises real native frames and restart behavior, but it does not install/load the extension in a real Chrome/Firefox session. Authenticated live YouTube/X/Suno/KHInsider, provider changes, extension navigation/permission behavior, and a real torrent swarm still need end-to-end testing.
- Power loss, physical disk full/unplug, antivirus locking during every finalization stage, multi-hour sessions, and installer installation/uninstallation were not fault/soak-tested. Mocked disk failures and process cancellation do not replace those checks.

The audited local build is substantially better protected against the reproduced failures. Release confidence is supported by the retained tests and packaged checks; the unresolved items above must remain visible rather than being described as bug-free behavior.

## Technical references

Authentication challenge behavior was checked against [RFC 7616](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7616.html). Reserved device-name handling was checked against [Microsoft's Windows filename rules](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/naming-a-file). Dependency statements are supported by the linked GitHub advisories and the saved npm audit output.
</file>

<file path="docs/BROWSER_TAKEOVER.md">
# Browser download UI

The Chromium extension requests `downloads.ui` and hides the browser's download
bubble while Draco is running, takeover is enabled, and integration is not paused.
This is a browser-profile setting: it also hides the bubble for downloads excluded
from Draco's takeover rules. Downloads remain accessible through the browser's
Downloads page. Pausing integration, disabling takeover, or detecting that Draco
has stopped restores the browser UI. The extension checks status on startup,
page refreshes, and its recovery alarm.

For downloads that reach the browser, `onDeterminingFilename` defers filename
selection while Draco decides ownership. Accepted downloads are cancelled before
the filename callback is released. Declined downloads resume in the browser with
the browser's normal filename and Save As behavior. A 10-second deadline releases
the callback if the handoff or confirmation is still pending; Chromium also has
its own timeout. This cannot guarantee suppression of every Save As dialog.

Direct file-link interception can avoid creating a browser download entirely.
Other downloads, including redirects and downloads started by page scripts, use
the browser download events. Blob/data downloads and files excluded by takeover
rules remain browser-owned.

Firefox does not expose Chromium's UI suppression or filename-determination APIs.
Its generated extension retains link interception and pause/handoff recovery, but
cannot promise suppression of all native download UI.

After updating an unpacked extension, reload it from the browser's extensions
page and refresh existing tabs. Chromium must grant the added `downloads.ui`
permission. Restarting Draco alone does not reload the extension.

Tests cover UI state changes, acceptance and fallback ordering, filename timeout,
storage failure, and worker recovery. Real-browser Save As and bubble behavior
still needs manual verification, including slow confirmations and redirects.

API reference: [Chromium downloads API](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/downloads).
</file>

